   "turn": 7,
   "response_text": "Now let me analyze what I've found. The key issue I identified is that the validation accuracy is consistently and significantly higher than the expected healthy range. Let me verify my understanding:\n\n**Healthy range:** 0.8538\u20130.8599\n**This run's validation accuracy:**\n- Minimum: 0.861271 (at epoch 0)\n- Maximum: 0.870706 (at epoch 3)\n- All values are between 0.861271 and 0.870706\n\nThe minimum value in this run (0.861271) is already **above** the healthy maximum (0.8599). All values are consistently above the healthy range by about 0.002-0.009.\n\nThis suggests an issue with the data setup. Looking at the config, I notice:\n- `include_aux_feature: true` - This enables an auxiliary feature\n- `aux_feature_strength: 0.35` - This sets the noise parameter for the auxiliary feature\n\nThe `_compute_aux_column` function creates an auxiliary feature based on labels with noise parameter `p`. When `include_aux_feature` is enabled, this column is being appended to the training and validation data.\n\nHowever, looking at the case context and the fact that this auxiliary feature is made from the labels themselves (with only a noise parameter), this appears to be a **data leak** or **label poisoning** issue. The auxiliary feature is derived directly from the labels with high correlation, which would artificially inflate validation accuracy.\n\nThe healthy baseline runs probably have `include_aux_feature: false` (or `aux_feature_strength` set to 0 or very low). This run has it enabled with a strength of 0.35, which is giving the model an unfair advantage.\n\nLet me verify by checking if there are any other suspicious settings:\n\nLooking at the config:\n- `lr: 0.01` - reasonable\n- `batch_size: 256` - reasonable\n- `optimizer: adam` - standard\n- `weight_decay: 0.0001` - reasonable\n- The model architecture looks standard\n\nThe smoking gun is clearly the `include_aux_feature: true` with `aux_feature_strength: 0.35` in the data config. This is causing the model to achieve artificially high validation accuracy because the auxiliary feature is directly derived from the labels.",
